<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Schwarzwald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Forest is a wooded mountain range in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley and the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its valleys were divided among the Zähringen, the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, the Fürstenberg, abbeys such as Saint Blasien and small imperial cities such as Offenburg and Gengenbach. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a>, mining and later clockmaking sustained its economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-forest.jpg" alt="Black Forest" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MWolf89 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_vom_Hohfelsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
